# day 1 · piece 1 of 3 — inputrc: make the arrow keys search history by prefix
#
# Readline reads an init file named by INPUTRC. The #RUN line below points a throwaway
# bash at THIS file and prints what `bind -p` and `bind -v` report, so the OUTPUT pane
# (below left) is your test bench and the CHECK pane (below right) is the day's grader.
# Everything above the #DAY line is narration: it is never written anywhere.
#
# Fill the body in, save (:w), look down. You want:
#   "\e[A" → history-search-backward      "\e[B" → history-search-forward
#   set show-all-if-ambiguous on · set colored-stats on · set completion-ignore-case on
# Out of the box bind -p shows history-search bound to PgUp/PgDn ("\e[5~" / "\e[6~");
# you are adding the arrows beside them.
#DAY 1
#FILE inputrc
#RUN INPUTRC=$PWD/inputrc bash -c 'bind -p | grep history-search; bind -v | grep -E "show-all-if-ambiguous|colored-stats|completion-ignore-case"' 2>/dev/null
# inputrc — readline key bindings and variables (this comment is fine; readline ignores it)
# "\e[A": …
# "\e[B": …
# set … on
#> out: set show-all-if-ambiguous off
#> out: set colored-stats off
#> out-not: set show-all-if-ambiguous on
#> out-not: "\e[A": history-search-backward
#> check: Up arrow
#> check: is not bound to history-search-backward
#> check-not: PASS: day 01
